      "text": "To determine the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) in the given right prism, we need to find the lengths of the sides \\(XY\\), \\(YZ\\), and \\(ZX\\). Let's start by identifying the coordinates of the points \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\).\n\nThe vertices of the base triangle \\(ABC\\) are:\n- \\(A = (0, 0)\\)\n- \\(B = (12, 0)\\)\n- \\(C = (6, -6)\\)\n\nThe height of the prism is 16, so the vertices of the top triangle \\(DEF\\) are:\n- \\(D = (6, -22)\\)\n- \\(E = (0, -16)\\)\n- \\(F = (12, -16)\\)\n\nThe midpoints \\(X\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(Z\\) of the edges \\(AC\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(DC\\) respectively are:\n- \\(X = \\left( \\frac{0+6}{2}, \\frac{0-6}{2} \\right) = (3, -3)\\)\n- \\(Y = \\left( \\frac{12+6}{2}, \\frac{0-6}{2} \\right) = (9, -3)\\)\n- \\(Z = \\left( \\frac{6+6}{2}, \\frac{-6-22}{2} \\right) = (6, -14)\\)\n\nNow, we calculate the distances \\(XY\\), \\(YZ\\), and \\(ZX\\).\n\n1. The distance \\(XY\\):\n\\[\nXY = \\sqrt{(9-3)^2 + (-3+3)^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 + 0^2} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n2. The distance \\(YZ\\):\n\\[\nYZ = \\sqrt{(9-6)^2 + (-3+14)^2} = \\sqrt{3^2 + 11^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 121} = \\sqrt{130}\n\\]\n\n3. The distance \\(ZX\\):\n\\[\nZX = \\sqrt{(6-3)^2 + (-14+3)^2} = \\sqrt{3^2 + (-11)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 121} = \\sqrt{130}\n\\]\n\nThe perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) is the sum of these distances:\n\\[\nXY + YZ + ZX = 6 + \\sqrt{130} + \\sqrt{130} = 6 + 2\\sqrt{130}\n\\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of triangle \\(XYZ\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6 + 2\\sqrt{130}}\n\\]",
